from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-4259';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 2: normalized Hamming distance and nonuniform algorithms; Definition 3; Section 6.1, Fact 25, Proposition 26 and Open Problem 3.'
refs += [dict(id='journal',title='An adaptivity hierarchy theorem for property testing — journal publication',authors='Clément L. Canonne; Tom Gur',year=2018,url='https://doi.org/10.1007/s00037-018-0168-4',locator='Computational Complexity 27:671–716; online 24 May 2018, issue December 2018. Publisher abstract and metadata checked; full journal text was not obtained. The Cambridge repository attachment under this journal record is the 25-page conference-format paper, not the 46-page version of record.'),dict(id='mirror',title='An adaptivity hierarchy theorem for property testing — institutional attachment',authors='Clément L. Canonne; Tom Gur',year=2017,url='https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/items/73b70279-d662-43f0-b591-98afd3ce529c',pdf_url='https://www.repository.cam.ac.uk/bitstreams/79801d54-9763-4371-9934-bbfd1e89ed77/download',locator='Attachment checked directly: conference-format text, Section 6.1 retains Open Problem 3. The repository’s journal metadata is not a later revision date for this file.')]
issue='Open Problem 3 requests an improvement of the universal exponential simulation but does not prescribe a candidate φ, a required asymptotic saving, or a joint limiting regime for q,k,ℓ. In particular, it does not state whether any improvement, improvement over iterating Proposition 26, or an optimal bound is the intended completion criterion. A quantitative benchmark target remains to be fixed; no sharper theorem has been invented here.'
notes=['Restored the Boolean value-query model, normalized distance, two-sided success and the k+1-batch convention.','Recovered the missing baseline 2^{q_k}−1 and the already-proved one-round reduction q_k(1+2^{q_k/k}).','Kept adaptive batch sizes and worst-case query counts; a fixed schedule is a stricter model.','Recorded the missing quantitative meaning of improvement and the journal-versus-repository attachment limitation.']
save(i,dict(title='Query cost of reducing testing adaptivity',formal='For testing an arbitrary Boolean property, how much can the universal query bound improve when a k-round-adaptive tester is replaced by an ℓ-round-adaptive tester, with 0≤ℓ<k? Writing q_j for the minimum query complexity at the same input length and proximity parameter, the source asks for a bound q_ℓ≤φ(q_k,ℓ,k) better than the general simulation q_ℓ≤2^{q_k}−1 when ℓ>0. A one-round reduction q_{k−1}≤q_k(1+2^{q_k/k}) is already proved. The source does not specify the amount or regime of improvement required of a general φ, so the quantitative target remains unfinished.',definitions='''Fix an integer n≥1, a nonempty property P⊆{0,1}ⁿ, and a proximity parameter ε∈(0,1]. An input f:[n]→{0,1} is available through a value oracle: a query i returns the single bit f(i). Its distance from P is min_{g∈P}|{i:f(i)≠g(i)}|/n. The tester must accept every f∈P with probability at least 2/3, and reject every input at distance strictly greater than ε with probability at least 2/3. On other inputs either verdict is allowed. Probabilities are over the tester’s internal random bits. This is ordinary two-sided testing with uniform Hamming distance, not tolerant testing, distribution testing or sample-only access.

Under the source’s convention, j rounds of adaptivity mean j+1 batches Q₀,…,Q_j. Each batch is chosen using internal randomness and the answers to all earlier batches. All locations in the current batch must be chosen before any of its answers are revealed. Batches may be empty. Both the identities of locations and the number of queries in a later batch may depend on earlier answers. Thus j=0 is a fully nonadaptive tester with one batch; j=1 permits two batches. The total number of value queries, summed over all batches and bounded on every input and random tape, is the cost. Local computation, descriptions of the property and query addresses are not charged as additional queries. Duplicate queries can be avoided or answered from stored values.

For this fixed n,P,ε, let q_j(n,P,ε) be the smallest worst-case number of queries used by a j-round-adaptive tester satisfying those guarantees. Algorithms are nonuniform in the source: for each n and known property they may be chosen separately. No polynomial running-time or uniform construction of testers from a succinct property description is required. Query budgets can always be capped at n by reading the entire input; extra empty rounds are harmless. The desired bound is universal over these Boolean properties and uses the same ε on both sides. Its function φ is to depend only on q_k,ℓ,k, with n,P,ε entering only through q_k.

Section 6.1 proves q_k≤q_ℓ≤2^{q_k}−1 for 0≤ℓ≤k and q_{k−1}≤q_k(1+2^{q_k/k}) for k>0. The first exponent is q_k; it is not the linear expression 2q_k−1. These are known universal upper bounds, not the unanswered result. Iterating the one-round inequality also supplies a bound for several removed rounds; the source’s wording does not select an explicit quantitative improvement over that consequence. Its remark stresses that general testers can adapt their batch sizes, so a theorem assuming a predetermined size for each batch would address only a special case.

The remaining task is to specify which joint regime of q_k,k,ℓ is being compared and what counts as a sufficient asymptotic improvement. The source does not demand matching lower bounds for an extremal tradeoff function, nor does it state a particular exponent-saving conjecture. This card therefore does not certify either of those stronger replacements.''',question_type='asymptotic_complexity',answer_criterion='A completed statement must specify the asymptotic upper bound φ and the regime and precision in which it improves the known simulation. A valid universal result must preserve the property, proximity parameter, success probability, adaptive batch-size freedom and worst-case query convention. The existing one-round theorem alone is not new progress; an optimal upper-and-lower tradeoff would be a stronger task than the source explicitly states.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Rounds and queries are different resources',text='A batch allows parallel access to many input positions. Reducing the number of batches may require more queries because later choices can no longer use as much information from earlier answers.',citation='primary'),dict(title='One removed round is already understood by an upper bound',text='Proposition 26 supplies a universal cost for deleting one round. Open Problem 3 asks for a more general dependence on the number of rounds retained, and the preceding remark keeps batch sizes adaptive.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The archived versions require care',text='The institutional record links the 2018 journal publication, but its downloaded attachment uses the conference layout and repeats the same broad open question. It does not justify claiming that the full journal text or a later quantitative resolution was checked.',citation='mirror')],why='Quantifies the general price of replacing sequential information gathering by fewer batches. A substantive universal bound would apply across Boolean property testing, beyond the behavior of one particular property.',progress=[dict(date='2017',text='The conference paper proves the exponential simulation and the one-round bound, then poses Open Problem 3 for a general reduction.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2018-05-24',text='A journal version was published; its abstract and metadata were checked, while the accessible institutional attachment remains conference-format.',citation='journal')],references=refs,status='uncertain',status_note='Read the complete conference question and both surrounding upper bounds, checked the institutional attachment and journal metadata, and searched subsequent work through 12 September 2026. No precise later formulation or general resolution was identified. The unspecified amount of improvement prevents a certified open-or-resolved verdict for one benchmark proposition.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,review_note=issue,statement_review=dict(reviewed_on=DATE,status='needs_specification',notes=notes,remaining_issue=issue,scope='Source-based formulation and status review; no substituted conjecture or independent proof audit.'),importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A general resource tradeoff for adaptive Boolean property testing; a precise quantitative target is needed to distinguish a substantive advance from the existing simulations. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The source asks how query complexity changes when a tester is allowed fewer batches of queries.','Its convention counts k rounds of adaptivity as k+1 actual batches.','A general exponential simulation and a better bound for removing one round are already known.','The requested new bound must cover arbitrary properties and allow the number of queries in each batch to depend on earlier answers.','The source leaves the amount and asymptotic regime of the desired improvement unspecified, which this card records explicitly.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','journal','mirror'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')
